    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M6 V10: Faraday's law. What changes create an induced emf? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "Here's what's going on. As the magnet comes closer, more of its field passes through the coil, so the flux through the coil changes. A changing flux pushes charges around the coil. That push is called an induced emf, short for electromotive force. Despite the name, it isn't a force. It's the energy given to each coulomb of charge that goes around, measured in volts, just like a voltage. Because the circuit is closed, the emf drives a current, and the needle swings. When the magnet stops, the flux stops changing, so the emf drops to zero, even though plenty of flux still passes through the coil. Now give the coil twice as many turns. The same magnet, pushed at the same speed, gives twice the emf. Each turn has the same flux through it, and each turn adds its own emf, like cells joined in series. So what really matters is the flux through one turn, times the number of turns. That's called the flux linkage, N times phi. Flux is what passes through one turn. Flux linkage counts it for every turn. Put those ideas together, and you get Faraday's law. The induced emf is minus N, times the change in flux, divided by the time the change takes. Epsilon equals minus N delta phi over delta t. A bigger change, more turns, or a shorter time, all give a bigger emf. The minus sign tells us which way the emf pushes, and that's next video's job. And one weber per second is one volt.",
